const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is a PMP certification worth it in India?',
    a: 'It is worth it once you already have close to the required 36 months (with a degree) or 60 months (without one) of real project-leading experience, especially in IT, infrastructure, or consulting, where employers explicitly screen for it at the mid-to-senior level. It is not worth pursuing in your first year or two on the job, since you are not yet eligible to sit the exam and a resume with zero delivery track record will not be rescued by a certification alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between a project manager and a product manager?',
    a: 'A project manager delivers a defined piece of work on time, on budget, and within scope, across industries like IT, construction, consulting, and manufacturing. A product manager decides what a product should do and why, based on customer and business signals, mainly in software and tech companies. They are different careers with different core skills: delivery discipline and stakeholder coordination for project management, versus customer judgment and prioritisation for product management. If you were actually searching for the product side of this decision, see how to become a product manager, product manager skills roadmap for India, or how to get a PM job after engineering.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many years of experience do I need for PMP eligibility?',
