        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>
  <p>
    If you are still building your first few years of real project experience, put your energy into
    coordinating one visible piece of work end to end, not into a PMP prep course you cannot yet
    register for. If you already have close to 3 years of project-leading experience, start the 35-hour
    education requirement now and time the exam once your hours are actually documented.
  </p>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Run yourself through The 4-Checkpoint Protocol above, honestly, on paper.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then map your real experience hours against the PMP eligibility table before spending on any
      training course.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Moving toward earlier financial freedom in a project management career comes down to a genuine
    <strong>high-income skill portfolio</strong> — delivery discipline, stakeholder trust, and the right
    certification timed correctly — not a certificate collected too early. If you want a second opinion
    on whether this path, or the certification timing, fits your situation,
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    can help you map it out, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>
    if you are still deciding between project management and a related track.
  </p>

